    },
    "sources": {
      "grounding": {
        "generic": [
          {
            "url": "https://www.politico.com/story/2018/05/21/trump-trade-china-600781",
            "title": "Trump victory lap ignores trade time bomb - POLITICO",
            "snippets": [
              "# Trump victory lap ignores trade time bomb\nPresident Donald Trump on Monday declared at least partial victory in his ongoing trade dispute with China and markets celebrated with the Dow spiking over 300 points as fears of a bruising clash between the world’s two largest economies eased.\n\nThe reality behind the scenes is significantly less rosy",
              "“In the long-term there is a 100 percent chance that the trade war reignites because we have not crafted anything close to a long-term solution,” said Derek Scissors, a China expert at the American Enterprise Institute. “There is also real risk in the short-term because this agreement is very unstable in terms of what the U.S. is actually going to gain. The Chinese may ultimately not give up anything the president can actually use politically.”",
              "Trump did inject a note of caution, tweeting that “China must continue to be strong & tight on the Border of North Korea until a deal is made.”\n\nBut markets largely embraced both the weekend headlines and Trump’s enthusiastic response, betting that a deeply feared back-and-forth tariff battle with the Chinese that could slam U.S. consumers, manufacturers and exporters will now not materialize",
              "On Sunday morning, Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin declared on Fox News that “we’re putting the trade war on hold” sending Asian markets higher and setting the stage for a Wall Street rally. But on Sunday evening, Lighthizer issued a statement that did not dispute that tariffs are on hold but took a seemingly much sharper line",
              "Those words sharply contrasted with the sunnier views offered on Monday by Mnuchin and National Economic Council Director Larry Kudlow who together represent the more staunchly pro-free trade wing of the White House.\n\n“I think we’re just rolling,” Kudlow said to reporters at the White House when asked whether there would now be a pause in negotiations. “I don’t see any pause at all. I would say there’s momentum building.”",
              "And that is where China observers see the greatest risks.\n\nFor now, Trump’s threat to put as much as $150 billion in tariffs on Chinese exports to the U.S.—and China’s threat to retaliate—is on hold. But it could easily come back if the Chinese drag their feet on actions that could actually reduce the $375 billion trade deficit the U.S. has with China, which is the president’s biggest concern",
              "But if the Chinese determine that the U.S. has failed to deliver on the one thing they want the most, they could back out of any agreement, putting the trade war back into effect. “You saw what happened with Trump’s ZTE tweet just a week ago and how much blowback he got from that,” Scissors said. “There are just so many ways this deal could blow up.”"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2018/05/20/mnuchin-says-trade-war-hold-after-progress-u-s-china-talks/627086002/",
            "title": "Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin says trade war is 'on hold' after ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin says trade war is 'on hold' after progress in U.S.-China talks\nUpdated May 20, 2018, 3:07 p.m. ET\n\nWASHINGTON — President Trump’s treasury secretary said Sunday the U.S. will not impose $150 billion in threatened tariffs on China while the two countries negotiate a deal to reduce America’s trade deficit with its economic rival",
              "“We’re putting the trade war on hold,” Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin said on Fox News Sunday. “We have agreed to put the tariffs on hold while we try to execute” an agreement under which China would increase its purchases of U.S. goods.\n\nMnuchin and other Trump administration officials held talks last week with Chinese Vice Premier Liu He, focused on trying to resolve the trade dispute",
              "The Trump administration had threatened to impose up to $150 billion in tariffs on Chinese products, part of an effort to address the U.S.-China trade imbalance and to penalize China for forcing American companies to turn over technology in exchange for access to the Chinese market. China responded by targeting $50 billion in U.S. products",
              "But Mnuchin said Sunday that last week’s talks resulted in “very meaningful progress,” including an agreement from Chinese leaders to increase their purchase of American goods. He would not confirm reports that China had offered to increase its purchases by $200 billion.\n\n“We have specific targets, I’m not going to disclose what they are,” Mnuchin told Fox News’ Chris Wallace",
              "Mnuchin said Commerce Secretary Wilbur Ross will be traveling to China to follow up on “hard commitments” from the Chinese to increase purchases in the U.S. energy and agriculture sectors.\n\nMnuchin denied that the Chinese concessions were a result of Trump’s promise to help ZTE, a Chinese telecom giant, wiggle out of U.S. sanctions after it was found to be illegally selling equipment to Iran and North Korea",
              "Last weekend, Trump suggested he wanted the U.S. to go easy on ZTE, tweeting that he was working with Chinese President Xi Jinping “to give massive Chinese phone company, ZTE, a way to get back into business, fast.” In the tweet, Trump said he had instructed the Commerce Department to help ZTE because “too many jobs” were being lost in China",
              "“We didn’t agree to any quid pro quo,” Mnuchin said. “That was completely independent of our trade negotiations.”\n\nThe Treasury chief said all Trump did was ask Ross to look into the ZTE matter, and any changes made to that settlement will about “protecting American technology” and American jobs.\n\n“This is an enforcement issue,” Mnuchin said.",
              "{\"headline\":\"Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin says trade war is 'on hold' after progress in U.S.-China talks\",\"shortHeadline\":\"Mnuchin: Trade war 'on hold' after progress in U.S.-China talks\",\"datePublished\":\"2018-05-20T17:15:36Z\",\"dateCreated\":\"2018-05-20T17:15:36Z\",\"dateModified\":\"2018-05-20T19:07:09Z\",\"articleSection\":\"news\",\"publisher\":{\"name\":\"USA TODAY\"},\"author\":{\"jobTitle\":\"World Affairs Editor/National Security Reporter\",\"name\":\"Deirdre Shesgreen\"},\"keywords\":[\"access:free\",\"ssts:news:politics\",\"sstsn:Politics\",\"type:story\",\"tag:Economic Sanctions\",\"tag:Steven Mnuchin\",\"tag:Import tariffs\"],\"isAccessibleForFree\":true,\"isPartOf\":{\"name\":\"USA TODAY - Unlimited Digital Access\",\"productID\":\"usatoday.com:standard\"},\"metadata\":\"{\\\"id\\\":\\\"627086002\\\",\\\"siteCode\\\":\\\"USAT\\\",\\\"contentSourceCode\\\":\\\"USAT\\\",\\\"ssts\\\":\\\"news:politics\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"story\\\"}\"}"
            ]
